# No line-ending conversion. Anywhere. This is not a preference.
#
# Every change to the application is made by a script that asserts an EXACT occurrence count of an
# anchor string before replacing it, and pins the SHA-256 of the file before and after. Git's
# autocrlf would rewrite line endings on checkout, which changes both the anchor bytes and the hash -
# so a chain that replays byte-exact on one machine would refuse to apply on another, or worse,
# would apply somewhere it should not.
#
# This has already bitten once without git in the picture: a round 7 anchor was authored with CRLF
# against a file that is LF, and the guard caught it before any write. That guard is the last line of
# defence, and it should not be spending itself on damage git did in transit.
#
# -text tells git to store and check out every file byte for byte, with no translation in either
# direction. The application, the patch scripts, and the harnesses all depend on it.

* -text

# The shipped application is a binary as far as diffing is concerned: 8.5 MB on one line in places,
# with an embedded 3.5 MB Mermaid bundle. A textual diff of it is never useful and is expensive to
# produce. What it changed is recorded by the patch script that changed it.
releases/*.html -diff
tools/mermaid.min.js -diff

